parts.text:
The user proposes: instead of defining all AST nodes upfront, write the builder first and add node definitions to ast.h as you write each visit. They ask: (1) is that approach OK? (2) how to do the indented printing (ast_dump)?

I should:
1. Validate their approach: yes, that's exactly the "vertical slice" idea — iterate rule by rule. But note one caveat: builder methods return types, so when writing a visit you need the node type it returns; defining node + visit together is fine. Also, since C++ requires declarations before use, when `visitAdditiveExpression` calls `visit(child)` and casts to `Expr*`, the base `Expr` must exist. So at minimum start with base classes (Node/Expr/Stmt/Type/Item) and then add concrete nodes as you go. Also the "what type does this rule return" decision must be made per rule.

2. Explain ast_dump: how to do indentation printing. The core idea:
   - Functions `dump(const Node*, int indent)`, print `indent` spaces (e.g., 2 per level) + a one-line description of the node; then recursively call dump on children with `indent+1`.
   - Since their AST likely has base classes with `kind` enum, dump can switch on kind and static_cast, or each class can have a virtual `dumpSelf()`/`dump(os, indent)` method. Both work. Simplest for beginner: a switch on kind + static_cast, or virtual method on nodes.
   - Alternative: each Node has `virtual void dump(std::ostream&, int indent) const`; base helper `indent()` writes `std::string(indent*2, ' ')`.
   - Need `#include <sstream>` if returning string; or print directly to std::cout.
   - Should handle null pointers.
   - Example small code snippet: they asked "怎么做啊" — I should give a short example (allowed; they're asking for an example). Show one base helper + one node's dump or the switch pattern.

   Maybe best to show the simplest approach: free function `std::string dumpAST(const Crate*)`? Or virtual. Let me decide: given their node design (base + kind enum?), earlier I suggested `enum Kind`. A switch-based dumper:

   ```cpp
   void dump(const Node* n, int depth) {
       if (!n) return;
       std::cout << std::string(depth * 2, ' ');
       switch (n->kind) {
           case Node::BINARY: {
               auto* b = static_cast<const BinaryExpr*>(n);
               std::cout << "Binary " << b->op << "\n";
               dump(b->lhs, depth + 1);
               dump(b->rhs, depth + 1);
               break;
           }
           case Node::INT_LIT: ... 
       }
   }
   ```
   But switch + static_cast per node is verbose. Virtual method is cleaner:

   ```cpp
   struct Node {
       virtual ~Node() = default;
       virtual void dump(int depth) const = 0;   // 或 virtual void dump(std::ostream&, int)
   };

   void indent(int depth) { std::cout << std::string(depth * 2, ' '); }

   struct BinaryExpr : Expr {
       Expr* lhs; Expr* rhs; std::string op;
       void dump(int depth) const override {
           indent(depth);
           std::cout << "Binary " << op << "\n";
           if (lhs) lhs->dump(depth + 1);
           if (rhs) rhs->dump(depth + 1);
       }
   };
   ```
   This is elegant: each node knows how to print itself. But it mixes printing into AST classes (fine for debug; some prefer separation). For a beginner, virtual dump is easiest to write incrementally (add node → add its dump). Recommend it.

   Also mention two-level indentation with `std::string(depth*2, ' ')`, recursion, null checks; and for one-line summaries like `Fn main`, print fields in the same line, children on following lines.

3. Note: if they use virtual dump in ast.h, they may need `<iostream>`/`<string>` includes; printing to std::cout directly is simplest. If they want testable output, take `std::ostream&`.

4. Also address the "parser 700 行全读" concern: they don't need to read all of Parser.g4; they need per-rule reference. Suggest workflow: use generated RxParser.h to see accessors, and read only the rule's line(s) in Parser.g4 when implementing that rule. Give a command to look up a rule: `sed -n '/^additiveExpression/,/;/p' grammar/Parser.g4` or grep.

5. Should I update the todolist doc? They asked two things: approach + dump how-to. Maybe add the dump recipe to now_todolist_2.md (section replacing/augmenting "打印函数自己写" line). Also update 1.5 to endorse incremental approach explicitly ("写一个 visit 补一个节点定义" is fine). Let me make a small edit to the doc:
   - In 1.5, add bullet: "也可以按你说的：写一个 visit 就补一个节点定义。但基类（Node/Expr/Stmt/Type/Item）+ Crate/Item/FnDef/Block 要先有，否则 builder 编译不过。"
   - Add a short "打印（dump）怎么做" subsection with the virtual-dump recipe and a small example (this is a third snippet, but user explicitly asked).

Let me keep the doc edit focused: add section "3.5 AST 缩进打印怎么做" after section 3? Or add to section 1.5. I'll add a new subsection after 1.5: "1.6 ast_dump 缩进打印怎么做". With a small code example (~15 lines).

Also correct their claim "parser 接近 700 行" — Parser.g4 is 697 lines. Right.

